<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Geotechnical Engineering in Kingston Ontario</title><meta name="description" content="Soil mechanics study in Kingston ON with lab and field testing. We analyze limestone till, clay, and dynamic"><link rel="canonical" href="https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Geotechnical Engineering in Kingston Ontario"><meta property="og:description" content="Soil mechanics study in Kingston ON with lab and field testing. We analyze limestone till, clay, and dynamic"><meta property="og:url" content="https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Geotechnical Engineering"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:image" content="https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/img/estudio-mecanica-suelos-1.webp"><meta property="og:image:width" content="1200"><meta property="og:image:height" content="630"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:title" content="Geotechnical Engineering in Kingston Ontario"><meta name="twitter:description" content="Soil mechanics study in Kingston ON with lab and field testing. We analyze limestone till, clay, and dynamic compaction fills. CA$4,640–CA$7,680. Accredited to ISO 17025."><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/img/estudio-mecanica-suelos-1.webp">
<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "LocalBusiness", "@id": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/#org-c2af", "name": "Kingston Ontario", "url": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/", "email": "info@geotechnical-engineering.org", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "addressLocality": "Kingston Ontario", "addressCountry": "Canada"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 44.3054, "longitude": -76.4284}, "areaServed": {"@type": "City", "name": "Kingston Ontario"}}, {"@type": "Service", "@id": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/#service-c2af", "serviceType": "Soil mechanics study", "name": "Geotechnical Engineering in Kingston Ontario", "description": "Soil mechanics study in Kingston ON with lab and field testing. We analyze limestone till, clay, and dynamic compaction fills. CA$4,640–CA$7,680. Accredited to ISO 17025.", "provider": {"@id": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/#org-c2af"}, "areaServed": {"@type": "City", "name": "Kingston Ontario"}, "url": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/"}, {"@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/", "url": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/", "name": "Geotechnical Engineering in Kingston Ontario", "inLanguage": "en", "isPartOf": {"@id": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/#website-c2af"}}, {"@type": "WebSite", "@id": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/#website-c2af", "url": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/", "name": "Kingston Ontario", "inLanguage": "en"}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "@id": "https://kingston-ontario.geotechnical-engineering.org/#faq-c2af", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "How much does a soil mechanics study cost in Kingston?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "For a typical residential or light commercial project in Kingston, the lab and field program ranges from CA$4,640 to CA$7,680. The final cost depends on the number of boreholes, sample quantity, and the specific tests required. A full suite with triaxial and consolidation testing sits at the upper end."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How long does the lab testing take?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Standard classification tests like grain-size and Atterberg limits take three to five business days. Consolidation and triaxial tests run longer, typically two to three weeks, because of the time needed for saturation and slow loading stages."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What's the biggest geotechnical risk in the Kingston area?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The abrupt transition from shallow limestone bedrock in the central peninsula to deep compressible clay east of the Cataraqui River. Underestimating the clay thickness leads to excessive settlement and costly foundation redesign."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do you test for Leda clay sensitivity?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. In areas like Collins Bay and along the waterfront where Leda clay is present, we run unconfined compression tests on undisturbed samples and measure the remolded strength. A sensitivity above eight triggers special excavation protocols."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What standards do you follow for a soil mechanics study?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Our lab operates under ISO 17025 accreditation and follows ASTM methods for all testing: D6913 for grain-size, D4318 for Atterberg limits, D2435 for consolidation, and D4767 for triaxial. Reports reference the NBCC 2020 where applicable."}}]}]}</script>
